From: Yocto build fix
Subject: [PATCH] autoapp: render video into the panel's real visible area, fix focus

This board's HDMI scanout is forced to a 1280x720 CEA timing -- the
only mode this hardware's video output (VOP) will actually activate;
the physical panel has no real onboard scaler and is natively smaller.
On-screen testing this session showed the panel only ever displays
roughly the first 1024x600 of whatever larger frame it receives --
top-left anchored, not centered or scaled -- which matches this
panel's real EDID-reported native resolution exactly, and also matches
its touchscreen controller's own hardware calibration range (confirmed
via Qt's evdevtouch device discovery log: "min X: 0 max X: 1024",
"min Y: 0 max Y: 600").

Filling the entire 1280x720 frame therefore means content drawn near
the right/bottom edges of what Android Auto sends lands in the region
the panel never displays. setFullScreen(true) cannot be used to render
into a deliberately smaller area: it always re-expands the widget to
the *entire* screen's geometry regardless of any geometry set
beforehand (QVideoWidget::setFullScreen -> showFullScreen(), defined
to cover the whole screen). Manage geometry directly instead, into a
fixed real-panel-sized box matching kPanelVisibleGeometry here -- which
must stay in sync with the identical geometry ServiceFactory now hands
to InputDevice (see the paired ServiceFactory.cpp change) so touch
coordinates line up with what's actually drawn.

Losing setFullScreen()/showFullScreen() also loses the window
activation it provided implicitly. Without an explicit
raise()+activateWindow(), this widget was observed to never actually
receive input focus on this EGLFS backend -- touch appeared completely
dead (not just miscalibrated) even though the widget was visibly on
screen and Qt's evdevtouch plugin had successfully initialized the
touch device. Separately, an earlier version of this fix called
setWindowFlags() from onStartPlayback(), i.e. on the widget *after* it
had already been created/shown -- changing window flags on an
already-realized top-level widget makes Qt destroy and recreate its
native window, which compounded the focus problem. Flags are now set
exactly once in createVideoOutput(), before the widget's native window
is ever created.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [oe-specific: workarounds for a
scaler-less display panel and an EGLFS window-focus/activation issue,
both observed on this board's Qt eglfs/KMS backend]

diff --git a/src/autoapp/Projection/QtVideoOutput.cpp b/src/autoapp/Projection/QtVideoOutput.cpp
index ad4636c..72da9db 100644
--- a/src/autoapp/Projection/QtVideoOutput.cpp
+++ b/src/autoapp/Projection/QtVideoOutput.cpp
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 */
 
 #include <QApplication>
+#include <QScreen>
 #include <f1x/openauto/autoapp/Projection/QtVideoOutput.hpp>
 #include <f1x/openauto/Common/Log.hpp>
 
@@ -43,6 +44,12 @@ void QtVideoOutput::createVideoOutput()
 {
     OPENAUTO_LOG(debug) << "[QtVideoOutput] create.";
     videoWidget_ = std::make_unique<QVideoWidget>();
+    // Set window flags here, once, before the widget's native window has
+    // ever been created/shown. Calling setWindowFlags() on an
+    // already-realized top-level widget (as onStartPlayback() used to)
+    // makes Qt destroy and recreate its underlying platform window, which
+    // was observed to be unreliable on this EGLFS/KMS backend.
+    videoWidget_->setWindowFlags(Qt::FramelessWindowHint | Qt::WindowStaysOnTopHint);
     mediaPlayer_ = std::make_unique<QMediaPlayer>(nullptr, QMediaPlayer::StreamPlayback);
 }
 
@@ -70,11 +77,36 @@ void QtVideoOutput::write(uint64_t, const aasdk::common::DataConstBuffer& buffer
 
 void QtVideoOutput::onStartPlayback()
 {
+    // This board's HDMI scanout is forced to 1280x720 (the only CEA timing
+    // this hardware's video output will activate -- the physical panel is
+    // natively smaller and has no real onboard scaler). On-screen testing
+    // this session showed the panel only ever displays roughly the first
+    // 1024x600 of whatever larger frame it receives -- top-left anchored,
+    // not centered or scaled -- matching this panel's real EDID-reported
+    // native resolution exactly. Render into that same real area (must
+    // stay in sync with the screenGeometry ServiceFactory hands to
+    // InputDevice, so touch coordinates line up with what's actually
+    // drawn) instead of the full 1280x720 the screen can physically scan
+    // out but the panel doesn't display.
+    //
+    // setFullScreen(true) (the previous approach here) always re-expands
+    // the widget to the *entire* screen's geometry regardless of any
+    // geometry set beforehand (QVideoWidget::setFullScreen ->
+    // showFullScreen(), defined to cover the whole screen), so it can't be
+    // used to render into a deliberately smaller area -- geometry/flags
+    // are managed directly instead. raise()+activateWindow() replace the
+    // window-activation showFullScreen() used to provide implicitly; without
+    // them this widget was observed to never actually receive input focus
+    // on this EGLFS backend (touch appeared completely dead, not just
+    // miscalibrated) even though it was visibly on screen.
+    static const QRect kPanelVisibleGeometry(0, 0, 1024, 600);
+
+    videoWidget_->setGeometry(kPanelVisibleGeometry);
     videoWidget_->setAspectRatioMode(Qt::IgnoreAspectRatio);
-    videoWidget_->setFocus();
-    videoWidget_->setWindowFlags(Qt::WindowStaysOnTopHint);
-    videoWidget_->setFullScreen(true);
     videoWidget_->show();
+    videoWidget_->raise();
+    videoWidget_->activateWindow();
+    videoWidget_->setFocus();
 
     mediaPlayer_->setVideoOutput(videoWidget_.get());
     mediaPlayer_->setMedia(QMediaContent(), &videoBuffer_);
